54. Industrial Finance and Accounting
Paper: Industrial Economics (ECNDSE303) | Unit: IV | Topic: Need for finance, types and sources of finance, commercial and development banks, internal vs external funding; accounting basics, balance sheet, profit and loss account, cost of capital, depreciation, cost accounting
Introduction
Finance is the lifeblood of industry. Firms need funds for setting up, expanding, buying raw materials and meeting daily expenses. Accounting records and reports the resulting financial position.
Need for finance
Fixed capital (land, plant, machinery), working capital (inventory, receivables, cash), expansion and modernisation, R&D, and meeting contingencies.
Types of finance
• Short-term (up to one year): trade credit, bank overdrafts, cash credit, commercial paper, factoring.
• Medium-term (1-5 years): term loans, lease financing, hire purchase.
• Long-term (over 5 years): equity shares, preference shares, debentures and bonds, term loans from financial institutions.
Sources
• Internal: retained earnings, depreciation provisions, reserves. Cheap, no control dilution, but limited.
• External: share capital, debt, bank loans, venture capital, private equity, foreign borrowings (ECBs), FDI, public deposits, IPOs.
Commercial and development banks
Commercial banks provide working capital and short- to medium-term loans. Development banks (IDBI, IFCI, SIDBI, NABARD, EXIM Bank, state financial corporations) offer long-term project finance, underwriting and promotional services. India moved from DFIs to universal banking after the 1990s (ICICI, IDBI converting to banks); NaBFID (2021) is a new infrastructure DFI.
Choice of funding: internal versus external
Pecking order theory (Myers): firms prefer internal funds, then debt, then equity. Trade-off theory: balance tax shield of debt against distress costs. Modigliani-Miller theorem: in perfect markets capital structure is irrelevant; with taxes, debt adds value. Factors: cost, risk, control, flexibility, market conditions, and tax.
Accounting basics
• Need: record transactions, measure performance, ensure accountability, support decisions, comply with law (Companies Act, Ind AS).
• Basic procedure: journal, ledger, trial balance, final accounts.
• Balance sheet: assets = liabilities + equity at a date; shows financial position.
• Profit and loss account: revenues minus expenses for a period; shows profitability.
• Cost of capital: the weighted average of costs of equity (CAPM or dividend model), debt (after tax) and preference capital: WACC = (E/V) Re + (D/V) Rd (1 - T). It is the discount rate in project appraisal.
• Depreciation accounting: allocation of an asset's cost over its life: straight-line, written-down value, units of production; recognises wear and tear and funds replacement.
• Cost accounting: classifying and controlling costs (fixed, variable, direct, indirect), standard costing, marginal costing and break-even analysis, for pricing and control.
Conclusion
Sound financing choices and transparent accounts reduce cost of capital, enable growth and build investor confidence.